Durarara!! AU
Chapter 22 – Part 2 
The confession lasted exactly as long as Curtis could stand being looked at after it.
He got up.
Red cap tipping, long black braids shifting against the black shirt, the old exit already forming in his mouth like weather.
“I’m going for a cigarette.”
Anri grabbed him.
Not hard. Not a fight. Just her hand closing around his wrist before the white suspenders and the rest of him could turn the later hour into a roof and a locked-down afterthought. The new Nikes stayed planted. Her tea sat abandoned. She had heard I like you a lot and she was not letting that sentence walk outside to get smoked into something smaller.
“Curtis-kun—”
His name came out sharp with panic and something warmer under it. Her fingers stayed on his wrist.
“Don’t. Don’t do that. You don’t get to say that and then leave the room like the next honest thing has to happen on a ledge.” A shaky breath. “You can smoke after. You can smoke in a minute. Just—stay. Please. I heard you. I’m still hearing you. Don’t turn it into a cigarette just because you actually said it.”
She did not pull him down onto her. She only held the wrist of the boy who liked her a lot and was already trying to put distance on the fact of it, the furnished flat suddenly too big for a retreat that small.
“Stay,” she said again, quieter. “Just stay.”
Curtis did not pull his wrist free.
He also did not sit back down. Red cap tipping, color still in his face, he looked at her hand on him like it had closed around more than fabric and skin.
“…Er. You know you’re making this really hard.” A rough little swallow. “Because I don’t often lay my heart out like that.”
The cigarette exit was still in his body. The confession was still in the room. Anri holding him there made both of them occupy the same second, which was exactly the part he did not have practice surviving.
Somewhere they could not hear, Justice leaned on the afterlife doorframe and snorted.
It’s true. He’s a fuckin’ wreck when it comes to women and his feelings. You should’ve seen him with his ex when he finally admitted how he felt for her. He was a mess. Too bad she turned out to be a horrible person to you.
The suite did not get that warning. Anri only got the boy in front of her, wrist caught, heart already half out and looking for a roof to hide on.
She kept her hold, gentler now that he had said hard instead of let go.
“…I know,” she said. “That’s why I’m not letting you take it outside and smoke it into something you can pretend was smaller.”
A thinner breath. Her thumb stayed against his wrist without tightening.
“You don’t have to say more. You already said you like me a lot. If laying that out is rare for you, then staying in the room after is the part that matches it. I’m not trying to trap you. I’m trying not to watch you run from the first true thing you’ve given me all day.”
She looked up at him under the red cap, still flushed, still holding on.
“If it’s hard, then sit with the hard part. Please. The cigarette can wait. This can’t, if you walk away from it right now.”
Unheard, Justice added, almost fond:
Yeah. Listen to her, idiot. The last time you laid it out and the room was unsafe, it wrecked you. This one is asking you to stay. That’s the difference.
Curtis sat down.
The cigarette stayed unlit. Anri’s hand slipped from his wrist as he sank back onto the couch, red cap tipping, the confession and the almost-escape both still occupying the same body. For a second the later hour almost became bearable again.
Then Anri could not hold the question in anymore.
“…Have you done stuff with Rio in here?”
Curtis’s face went flush so fast it made the earlier blush look mild.
“You can’t ask something like that, Anri-chan.” The protest came first, automatic and flustered. Then, because leaving it unanswered would make the room worse, he pushed through it. “But no. We have never done anything in that sense. She has simply come over, talked, and hung out. Nothing ever happened. No kissing. No hugging. Or what you were referring to.”
A shorter, rougher breath. He kept his eyes on the coffee instead of her.
“The last time I engaged in that activity was before I came to Japan.”
The furnished flat went quiet around the inventory. No Rio in his bed. No secret physical claim on the couch Anri was sitting on. Just talk, venting, the easier conversations of someone who had not watched him break a man in the street — and a history that still belonged to the cruel girl in California, not to anyone in Ikebukuro.
Anri’s shoulders loosened so visibly she looked almost ashamed of the relief.
“…Okay.” The word came out small. Then, hotter in the face, “I’m sorry. I shouldn’t have asked it like that. It sounded—ugly. I just kept seeing her in this room in my head after what you said, and I needed the actual shape of it.”
A thinner breath. Her hands stayed open in her lap.
“I’m glad nothing happened. I know that’s not a noble thing to be glad about. I still am.” She glanced at him under the red cap, embarrassed and unable to pretend otherwise. “Thank you for answering anyway. And for sitting back down.”
The later hour held the confession, the grabbed wrist, and now this: Anri knowing the suite had not been that kind of place with Rio, and Curtis knowing she had needed that answer badly enough to ask it anyway.
Curtis dragged a hand down his still-flushed face, red cap tipping as a thin laugh escaped him.
“You’re going to give me a heart attack, Anri. And those can’t kill me. It’s just going to hurt really bad. Ahaha.”
The joke landed in the room with more weight than he seemed to notice. Confession, grabbed wrist, Rio question, now those can’t kill me dropped in like a punchline he expected her to treat as exaggeration.
Anri stared at him.
“…Don’t say it like that.” The scold came first, then the unease under it. “You just told me you like me, I asked something I shouldn’t have, and now you’re joking about a heart attack that can’t kill you. That isn’t as funny as you think it is.”
A shorter beat. Her eyes searched his face under the cap.
“If you mean I startled you, just say I startled you. You don’t have to put a laugh on getting hurt, and you don’t have to talk like pain is the only part of you that still counts.” She exhaled, softer despite herself. “I’m not trying to give you a heart attack. I’m trying to stay in the room with what you said. That’s already a lot. You don’t need to add… whatever that was.”
She did not ask whether can’t kill me was a joke or one of those true things he sometimes said too casually. Not yet. The later hour already had enough of him laid out without opening that door too.
A visible sweat mark appeared at Curtis’s temple under the red cap.
“…Er. Sorry.”
That was the whole defense. No second joke. No can’t kill me follow-up. Just the tall boy on his own couch, white suspenders and all, looking suddenly like he had said one true-adjacent thing too many and gotten caught by the one person he had just confessed to.
Anri watched the sweat mark, then the apology, and the last of her scold loosened into something closer to helpless fondness.
“…Good,” she said, quieter. “Sorry is better than another laugh.”
A small breath left her. She did not reach for the can’t kill me. She let er, sorry stand as the correction.
“You’re terrible at this,” she added, not unkindly. “Saying huge things, then joking, then sweating. Just stay sitting. I think that’s the safest version of you right now.”
Curtis gave a tiny, mortified nod, still flushed, still not going for the cigarette, the later hour officially more dangerous to his composure than any doctor’s dinner had been.
Curtis kept the mortified nod on his face and let the rest happen where Anri could not follow it.
Wow. Suddenly ignoring her call doesn’t seem so bad anymore.
The thought arrived unhelpful and honest. The later hour had gone from tea and not leftover to a confession, a grabbed wrist, a question about Rio, and a joke about a heart attack that could not kill him. Sitting still, as instructed, suddenly felt like the more dangerous option. A dead line on a sidewalk would have been quieter than this couch.
Justice did not let that thought live in peace.
’Cause you can’t talk to women. You are fuckin’ terrible at it.
The brother’s voice sat in the cheap seats again, delighted and appalled.
You can’t say those things to her, Curtis! “I like you a lot” was already a miracle. Then you follow it with durability comedy. She asked you not to laugh the pain off and you handed her a heart attack that won’t finish the job. That’s not charm. That’s a man sprinting from his own mouth.
Curtis’s sweat mark did not improve.
He stared at his coffee like it might offer a script Justice would have recited properly and came up with nothing. Anri was still on the couch. The Nikes were still on the floor. The confession was still in the room. And the version of him that wanted to ignore calls, climb roofs, and smoke sentences into smaller shapes was being held in place by a girl who had asked him, very plainly, to stay sitting.
Justice sighed, almost fond.
Don’t run. You already said the true thing. Now survive the silence after it like an adult, you disaster.
Anri watched him sweat through the silence a moment longer, then asked the question that had been sitting under every mention of the brother who could recite what Curtis could not.
“…Was it always just you and Justice?”
Curtis looked at her like the furnished room had tilted.
Red cap tipping, the mortified heat in his face cooled into something more startled than flustered. People had asked about Appalachia. About scars. About why he wandered. About the photograph. No one in Ikebukuro had gone for that exact shape: not what happened to him, not were you abandoned together, but whether the pair of them had been the whole of it.
“…That’s a question no one’s asked yet.”
He said it quietly, almost impressed despite himself. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as his mismatched eyes moved from her to the shelf of books that were actually his, then back.
Anri did not fill the pause. She had learned, in the last hour, that if she waited he sometimes came the rest of the way on his own.
Curtis’s thumb tapped once against the coffee cup.
“No,” he said at last. “It wasn’t always just us.” A short, careful beat. “There were others. Later. After we left the woods. I’m not— I don’t talk about them the same way. They’re not a story I put on a table.”
He did not name the Undead.
He did not say they were looking for him.
He did not say four more bags of money had once sat in the same split.
Anri heard the door crack and not fully open.
“…But there were more,” she said softly. “So Justice wasn’t your only person. He was just the first one who stayed.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged, not quite a smile.
“Yeah. Something like that.”
On the couch, the later hour picked up another piece of him that even Celty and Shinra had not thought to ask for — and he let it exist there, unfinished, because Anri had asked the question no one else had thought to ask yet.
Curtis turned the cup in his hands, red cap tipping as if the number itself needed a running start.
“I met Justice when I was five.”
Anri’s eyes widened.
“…Five?”
He grimaced.
The sound of it in someone else’s mouth made the fact less like a calendar and more like a wound with a date on it. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as he pushed the rest out before the flinch could close the door again.
“…So. I was abandoned in the woods when I was five, and I met Justice there.” A short, rough pause. “We met the others years later.”
He left it at that. Cabin to forest. Forest to a second abandoned boy. Years after that, more people — unnamed, undetailed, sitting in the same category as the question no one had asked until tonight.
Anri sat very still on his couch, new Nikes on the floor, the later hour suddenly holding a much smaller version of him than the tall boy in the red cap. Five. Woods. Another child already living in them. The confession I like you a lot was still in the room, but it had just been joined by the origin of the person who had said it.
“…Curtis-kun.” His name came out thin. “You were five.”
Not a question this time. A placement.
“I knew you were abandoned. I didn’t have the picture of it being that early, and that it started with another kid instead of a rescue.” Her hands stayed open in her lap, careful not to clench. “You don’t have to tell me the others tonight. I heard you. Five years old in the woods is already… a lot to sit next to.”
A quieter beat.
“Thank you for saying it anyway. I asked because I wanted the shape of you, not because I needed every name.”
She looked at him under the cap, the amazement at the furnished flat now tangled with the fact that most of this life had been built after a forest that had no business keeping a five-year-old.
Curtis’s mouth tightened under the red cap, the next piece coming out like something he had been carrying longer than the later hour.
“The others don’t know I’m here.” A short pause. “They probably know Justice is dead by now… but I left without saying goodbye.”
The furnished room took that in and got heavier.
Not just abandoned at five. Not just a brother who could recite what he could not. A whole unnamed set of people still somewhere behind him, likely already grieving one boy, and never told that the other one had crossed an ocean instead of coming back to the grief.
Anri’s face changed.
“…You left them with his death and no you.”
It was not an accusation so much as the shape of it spoken out loud. She saw the guilt immediately, sitting on him beside the blush and the confession and the sweat mark.
“Curtis-kun…” Her voice dropped. “That’s why the list, isn’t it? Why you talk like you’re passing through even in a place this finished. You didn’t just lose him. You disappeared on the people who would have had to lose both of you at once.”
A thinner breath. She did not ask for names. She did not ask where they were.
“If they loved you the way it sounds like they did, not saying goodbye is going to hurt them. It’s also something you can still fix one day. You don’t have to fix it tonight. I just—don’t want you sitting here calling that fine either.”
She looked at him, careful and present.
“You told me I won’t lose a place in you. Those people probably thought they had one too. That’s not me telling you to go back this second. That’s me hearing how much leaving without goodbye is still sitting in this room with us.”
Curtis shook his head once, red cap tipping as if Anri’s picture of him vanishing on grieving friends needed one correction and not the whole roster.
“No. They know.” A short, rough pause. “We all will keep it undead till the day we die. Like a dove and a grenade.”
The phrase left him the way the confession had: too plain to be poetry and too loaded to be slang.
Anri went still.
The later hour folded backward through Ikebukuro and landed on a street that already knew those words. A mask. A hood that came down. Braids falling forward. A crowd gathering around a boy who had just arrived in the city and sung like the song was not cover material, but a promise. Every single one of us is undead. This is for the ones that I bled with. I’ll be screaming I’m Undead ’til I die. She had stood in that crowd without knowing his name yet. Rio had recognized him later from the same night. And now, on his couch, he was saying the others knew — not where he was, maybe, but what the vow was.
“…That song.”
Anri’s voice came out thin with recognition.
“The first night you were here. The busking. You sang that. I didn’t understand it then. I thought it was just… a performance. A mask song.” Her eyes searched his face under the red cap. “It wasn’t. It was them. Justice. The others. The thing you keep even when you leave without goodbye.”
A smaller breath.
“A dove and a grenade.” She repeated it like she was turning the image over. “Soft and something that blows the room apart. That’s what you meant by the other half, isn’t it? The kind version and the one that ends a fight in the street. Both of them are the same promise. You just didn’t tell anyone here what the promise was attached to.”
Curtis did not deny it.
The guitar in the corner suddenly looked less like a hobby and more like the leftover instrument of a name he had already sung into the city before any of them knew to ask who the Undead were.
Anri’s hands stayed open in her lap.
“…So they know you’ll keep it. They just don’t know you kept it in Ikebukuro.”
The later hour held that too now: a confession, a forest at five, a brother who could recite, four unnamed others, and a street song that had been a vow the whole time.
Anri sat with the song still rearranging itself in her head: a street performance turning into a vow, a mask turning into people he had left behind. When she finally asked something, it was not for names.
“…If you all keep it undead until you die,” she said carefully, “then what is Ikebukuro supposed to be?”
Curtis looked at her.
She did not look away.
“I mean it. You left without goodbye. They don’t know you’re here. But you’re still singing the promise like it didn’t move countries.” A thinner breath. “So is this place part of keeping it… or is this place what you do until the list is finished and then you disappear again? I need to know which night I’m sitting in.”
The furnished flat held the question without decoration. Not who are they. Not can I meet them. The sharper one, the one that belonged to a girl who now had a place in him and had just learned that place existed beside a vow he had already sung to a crowd of strangers.
“Are you staying, Curtis-kun?” she asked, quieter. “Or are we all another city you can keep undead in without ever telling the people the promise was made with?”
Curtis leaned back, red cap tipping as the hard question turned into a memory instead of a clean yes or no.
“…Hmm. Now that’s a tough question.” A short breath. “This was supposed to be Justice and I’s final stop. Where we would just live our lives.”
Anri waited.
“He was always fascinated with manga, anime, Japanese culture. When he was seven he found picture books of Dragon Ball.” A faint, real huff of amusement crossed Curtis’s face. “Heh. He read the book wrong. We were Westerners and not used to reading manga.”
The smile thinned into something fonder and older.
“But then he found books on Japanese culture. Tourist cards from Japan. He always wanted to come settle down here.”
The furnished flat — chosen before death, paid years ahead, books on the shelf that were actually Curtis’s — suddenly had a second occupant in it again. Not as a ghost lecture. As a seven-year-old holding a volume backward, then growing into a boy who picked a country like a future instead of a hiding place.
Anri’s expression softened until it hurt.
“…So this apartment isn’t a coincidence.” She looked around the rooms he had not decorated, the life he had stepped into. “He wanted this. You brought him here anyway, even after.”
A quieter beat.
“That isn’t the same as answering if you’re staying. But it tells me why the idea of disappearing from here would feel like breaking more than a lease.” Her eyes came back to him under the red cap. “You were supposed to live. Not pass through. That’s what he wanted the city for.”
She did not force the yes out of him. She let the Dragon Ball book and the tourist cards sit on the couch with the vow and the confession, a final stop that had lost one of the people it was built for and still had the other one sitting in it, unsure how to occupy a future that was meant to be shared.
Curtis turned the cup once more, red cap shadowing the mismatched eyes as the tourist cards and the backward Dragon Ball pages gave way to the part that was only his.
“To be honest, Anri-chan… I just wanted to be where he was. I didn’t care where that was.” A short, rough pause. “But now I’m left to myself, and I don’t even know what I want. So I’m just going to stay and live out the years. And I’ll see if I ever visit the USA again.”
It was not a romantic promise to Ikebukuro. It was not a vow to disappear. It was the leftover architecture of a boy whose compass had been another person: stay in the city that was supposed to be their final stop, occupy the years because the years were what remained, and leave the question of going back across the ocean for a future tense he did not trust yet.
Anri heard the staying and the lostness in the same sentence.
“…That’s an honest answer,” she said quietly. “Not the one I wanted, maybe. The true one.”
A thinner breath.
“You stay because this is where he wanted to live, and because you don’t know what you want without him. That’s not the same as belonging to the city. It’s also not you telling me this is temporary until you vanish on us the way you vanished on them.” Her hands stayed open in her lap. “Living out the years is still staying, Curtis-kun. I’ll take that over a pretty speech about loving Ikebukuro.”
She looked at him under the cap, the confession and the forest and the Undead vow all sitting behind this plainer thing.
“If you don’t know what you want yet… then stay long enough to find out. That’s all I was asking. Not for you to be finished grieving. Just not to treat the years like a hallway you walk through without letting anyone keep a place in them.”
The later hour held that too: a boy who would remain, not because he had chosen a future, but because the person he followed was gone and the years still had to be lived somewhere.
Curtis’s mouth tugged under the red cap, the grief loosening just enough for a sharper, dryer air to get in.
“The people I do meet are complex. It’s interesting.” A short pause. “It’s a breath of fresh air to not be known, too. The look on Izaya’s face when he can’t find info on me is priceless.”
Anri blinked, thrown by the turn and then, despite herself, almost smiling at the image.
“…Orihara-san.” The name came out with the exact caution Masaomi had taught the whole group. “He looked at you like you were a puzzle he already wanted to take apart. I remember that.”
A smaller beat.
“I can see why not being in his files would feel like breathing.” Her eyes flicked over him, the boy with no clean paper trail, a forest for a childhood, and a vow sung to a street before anyone knew his name. “Just… don’t enjoy that part so much that you start disappearing on the people who aren’t trying to catalog you. Being unknown to Izaya is one thing. Being unknown on purpose to everyone else is how you end up alone in a nice apartment living out years you don’t know how to want.”
Curtis huffed, faint and unoffended.
“Yeah. That’s fair.”
The later hour let the joke about Izaya’s face sit beside the heavier stuff without erasing it: a rare clean pleasure in a city that kept trying to name him, filed next to the girl who already had a place in him and was asking him not to spend that anonymity on her too.
Curtis looked at her properly again, red cap tipping as the Izaya joke gave way to something warmer and more precise.
“You got some valuable info. But you’re so kind and loving, Anri-chan.” A short pause, mismatched eyes steady. “I know you would never turn on me. Which is why I feel silly for overreacting the last few days.”
Anri’s breath caught at kind and loving before the rest arrived.
“Talking with you feels soothing.” His voice dropped a fraction. “And I bet that blade is very quiet again now that you’re near me.”
The furnished room went still.
Not because of the compliment. Because of the last line. The underpass. The voice that had told her to cut him. The way he had heard it anyway, made the sword stop speaking, and then walked her partway home without demanding the whole secret. He was naming Saika without naming Saika — and he was saying the thing in her that wanted blood went quiet when she sat in his air.
Anri’s hands flexed once in her lap and stayed open.
“…Curtis-kun.” His name came out thin. “You shouldn’t say that so casually. Even if it’s true.”
A smaller breath. Color touched her face, tangled with relief she did not want to look at too directly.
“It is quieter.” She admitted it because lying after I like you a lot felt worse than the danger of the blade. “I hate that you can tell. I also—” her voice frayed, then steadied, “—I also don’t want you to think that’s the only reason talking to you feels safe. You’re not just a mute switch. You’re… soothing. Like you said. That’s why I came. Not only because the other voice gets smaller near you.”
She searched his face under the cap, earnest and shaken.
“Please don’t use that as another reason to keep me close if it goes wrong. Use the part where I wouldn’t turn on you. Use the part where this conversation actually helps. The blade being quiet can stay a side effect. Not the point.”
Inside her, for once, Saika did not argue.
The later hour held that silence too.
Curtis chuckled, shaking his head under the red cap before the fear in her voice could harden into the wrong shape.
“No, no, Anri-chan. I didn’t mean it like that.”
He reached over and took her hand.
The contact was simple. No grab. No performance. Just his palm over the fingers that had clenched and unclenched through the later hour, warm against the leftover tension of Rio, the blade, and are you staying. Long black braids shifted against the black shirt as he looked at her instead of the joke he had almost let her misunderstand.
“It’s okay. I like being close with you.”
Anri went very still, then the stillness broke in a small helpless way. Her hand stayed in his. The new Nikes did not move. The confession, the grabbed wrist, the forest at five, the vow, and now this — I like being close with you — landed softer than I like you a lot and somehow closer to the body.
“…Oh.”
The sound left her unguarded. Color rose in her face, but she did not pull away. Her fingers adjusted, carefully, so the hold became mutual instead of only his.
“I like being close with you too,” she got out, quieter than the hallway, quieter than the phone call, almost the same volume as the blade’s unusual silence. “You can hold on. I won’t take it as you using me for the quiet.”
She looked at their hands, then up at him under the cap, the later hour finally looking like what she had come for and not only what she had raced Rio to claim.
“Stay like this a minute. Please.”
Justice lost his mind in the cheap seats.
Bro! Do it!!
The dead boy’s voice had the same unhelpful volume it had always had in parking lots and stolen-cabin kitchens, zero respect for a tender moment and even less for tact.
You can do it! You can do it all night long—
A wheezing break, then the laugh that had always come when Curtis froze at the exact second courage was supposed to become motion.
Bahahahaha. You probably won’t, you dumb softy.
On the couch, Curtis did not do it.
He kept holding Anri’s hand. That was the whole campaign. Red cap tipping, ears still warm, thumb resting carefully against her fingers like closeness itself was already the line he had managed to cross. No lean-in. No all-night-long. Just the boy Justice had called a wreck with feelings, occupying the minute Anri had asked him to stay in.
Unheard, Justice groaned with theatrical betrayal.
Look at you. Confession. Hand. Tea. Tragic backstory. And the move is ‘stay like this a minute.’ You’re going to live out the years like a gentleman in a furnished flat and I’m going to die a second time from secondhand restraint.
Curtis, who could not hear him, only squeezed Anri’s hand once — soft, brief, the exact opposite of all night long — and stayed sitting, a dumb softy on purpose, while the later hour remained a held hand instead of a dare.
Anri’s fingers tightened once in his, eyes lowering to their joined hands as if the later hour had finally given her enough safety to name the mess she had carried in the door.
“…I’m sorry for everything.”
The hallway. The stare after the fight. Lecturing him over the shoes. Racing Rio to his address. Asking about what had happened in this room. Making him say the hard things and then flinching at the shape of them. It all sat in the one word.
Curtis shook his head before she could stack more onto it. Red cap tipping, he kept her hand instead of using the apology as a reason to let go.
“You don’t have to apologize.”
A short, quieter beat.
“You stared. I disappeared. You called. I almost didn’t answer. That’s the last few days. I’m not keeping a tab.” His mismatched eyes stayed on her face, softer than the ninth-circle version of him. “You’re here. You’re holding on. That’s enough for me tonight, Anri-chan. Don’t turn the whole evening into a debt.”
Anri’s breath loosened against the refusal. She did not take the sorry back out loud, but she let it sit unfinished, the way he had let the person who I— sit unfinished, and kept her hand in his.
After a while they settled in closer.
Not a leap. Just the natural close of two people who had already said too much to keep a polite gap. Anri’s shoulder found the line of his arm. Curtis let the red cap tip against the couch back, long black braids falling nearer her, their hands still loosely joined on the cushion between. The furnished flat finally resembled the quieter night he had marked on a calendar and never quite gotten.
Anri asked a simple question into that ease.
“…Why do you smoke?”
Curtis huffed a small, unguarded sound, as if he had expected something heavier after forests and vows and I like you a lot.
“Oh. There’s nothing special behind that. Rather an old habit.” A short pause. “When I was in L.A. I used to smoke weed. Justice warned me when we got to Japan I would have to quit, ’cause the laws are strict. I didn’t mind.”
His thumb traced once over her knuckles, absent.
“So I’m going to quit cigs eventually.”
Anri turned her face a little toward his shoulder, considering that. The boy who jumped off roofs for a smoke break, who had almost fled this confession for one, talking about the cigarette like it was only a leftover tool from a city with different rules.
“…Eventually is not never,” she said softly. “That’s better than ‘I need it to think.’” A smaller beat. “I’m glad you listened to him about the other thing. And I’m glad this isn’t some tragic reason. It’s just a habit you’re already planning to put down.”
She let the closeness stay.
“You don’t have to quit tonight. You just don’t have to use one as a door out of this room anymore.”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Yeah. I heard you the first time you grabbed my wrist.”
The later hour held that too: a simple question, a simple answer, and two people sitting closer than the start of the evening had any right to expect after a day of ruined shoes and unfinished fortunes.
They chuckled.
The sound was small and shared, the first easy thing the later hour had produced without a flinch attached to it. After that the night simply went on. Tea gone lukewarm. Conversation thinning into quieter pieces. The television on low and ignored. Two people occupying the furnished flat the way it had been meant to be occupied: not as a crisis room, not as a prize over anyone else, just a place to sit close and let the day finish without swallowing them.
Eventually Anri had to leave.
School in the morning. No change of clothes. The same practical boundary that had sent Rio home the night before — a parallel Curtis did not offer her, because the evening did not need that comparison spoken out loud. She stood, new Nikes finding the floor again, and the closeness on the couch became the smaller problem of a doorway.
Curtis walked her there, red cap still on, long black braids against the black shirt.
They hugged.
Not a polite exit hug. They held each other in the doorway, Anri’s hands closing in the white suspenders, his arms around her with the same careful certainty as when he had taken her hand. For a few seconds the hallway did not exist. Raira did not exist. Rio’s text and Mikado’s ruined shoes and the sushi-counter fortunes stayed outside the circle of it.
“…Good night, Curtis-kun,” she murmured into his shoulder.
“Good night, Anri-chan.”
They held on a little longer than a goodbye required, then let go because morning would not. Anri stepped back into the corridor with the new shoes and a place in him that no longer felt theoretical. Curtis stayed in the threshold until the building door downstairs shut, then closed his own, the cleaner evening look suddenly too dressed for an apartment that had just gone quiet again.
The later hour was over.
The years, as he had said, were still there to live out.
And for once the last thing in the room was not a cigarette, a lecture, or a fortune. It was the leftover warmth of a doorway hug he had not run from.